{
 "about": "Tesla vs Waymo robotaxi status by US metro. Hand-curated from each company's own pages, checked 25 Nov 2026. Statuses: live = public riders today; live-driver = public riders with a human safety driver; announced = the company named the city but no public rides yet. Hiring signals are computed at build time and kept separate.",
 "checkedAt": "2026-11-25",
 "definitions": {
  "tesla": {
   "live": "Public Robotaxi rides today (Tesla Robotaxi Safety Report page and launch reports). Bay Area counted separately as live with a safety driver.",
   "announced": "Cities Tesla lists as coming soon on its Robotaxi pages.",
   "hiring": "Cities with open Robotaxi Fleet Support Supervisor postings on Tesla's US careers site (this tracker's daily check)."
  },
  "waymo": {
   "live": "Cities on Waymo's rides page under Serving Riders In, including Austin and Atlanta (rides via Uber) and Las Vegas (riders added gradually).",
   "announced": "Cities on Waymo's rides page under Up Next. Waymo drives, maps or tests in these before opening, so this bucket includes testing-stage cities.",
   "hiring": "US metros named in Waymo's own open job postings (Greenhouse board waymo), any role, ignoring remote and catch-all postings that list 6+ cities. Waymo's board is mostly engineering and corporate roles, and some day-to-day fleet work is done by partners such as Moove, so this is a weaker signal than Tesla's."
  }
 },
 "notes": [
  {
   "text": "Waymo has partnered with Moove to run fleet operations in Phoenix and Miami.",
